WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.910
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Most people who

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:02.952
are familiar with paying
for things probably know

00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:04.180
what an invoice looks like.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.300
But you may also be aware of how
challenging deciphering a Cloud

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.790
invoice can be, potentially
with thousands of lines

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.830
of cryptic acronyms and text.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.690
On this episode and
"Beyond Your Bill,"

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.940
we'll walk you through how
to explore your costs using

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.148
Google Cloud Billing Reports.

00:00:17.148 --> 00:00:19.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:25.840
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Our last videos

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.900
covered how resources in your
GCP account can be organized

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.540
and how to manage permissions
with a billing account.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.100
In this video, we'll
look at how you

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.530
can explore the costs associated
with a billing account

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.353
by viewing billing reports
in the Google Cloud console.

00:00:38.353 --> 00:00:40.270
We'll focus on answering
some common questions

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.120
like how much am I spending,
what are my cost trends,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.010
and what are my cost drivers?

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.080
I'll use a sample billing
account for this video,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.940
but you can go to
the Cloud console

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.490
and use the left hand navigation
to click on Billing and then

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.100
Reports to view billing reports
for your own billing accounts

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.480
and projects.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.670
The report defaults to
the current calendar month

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.280
and shows you a lot of
information right off the bat.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.050
In the top left,
you can see the cost

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.038
to date for the
current month and how

00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:05.830
that compares to the
cost for the same time

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.780
period in the previous month.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.353
In the top right, you can
see the forecasted cost

00:01:10.353 --> 00:01:11.770
for the current
month and how that

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.662
compares to the cost
from the previous month.

00:01:13.662 --> 00:01:15.370
Right in the middle,
there's a chart that

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.350
shows cost group by project.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.280
Each color shows a
different project,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.580
and you can hover over the chart
to see more specific details.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.382
There's also a dotted
cost trend line

00:01:24.382 --> 00:01:25.840
that forecasts what
you're spending

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.050
might look like in the future
based on historical trends.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.490
Under the chart,
you'll see a list

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.390
of projects and a table view.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.630
In addition, there are
filters on the right side

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.968
to drill down and further
analyze your costs.

00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:39.010
You can customize the time
range you're currently

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.410
viewing so you can
explore previous months,

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.540
the past quarter, or any
other window of time.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.990
In addition, you can
toggle the invoice month

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.810
to line up the report
directly to your invoice.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.790
When looking at your
costs by invoice,

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.190
you'll be able to filter out
specific charges such as taxes.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.210
This is the best way to get
an exact visual representation

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.040
of the invoices
sent to your company

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.590
and to explore the costs
associated with each project

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:03.580
or service.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.320
You can also choose
from other filters

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.450
to see how your
costs are trending.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.280
As you change the
time range, the costs

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.200
and comparative
costs above the graph

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.090
will update based on your view.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.310
You're also able to
filter costs by location,

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.370
selecting a geography,
region, or multi-region area.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.410
This is especially handy to
see a cost breakdown by region,

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.550
such as comparing your costs
between the Americas and EMEA.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.130
Click a location to include
it in your filtered view

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:30.820
and deselect all the locations
to go back to the default view.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:32.980
There is also the ability
to selectively filter out

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:35.470
credits, which can be made
up of different discounts

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.380
based on your usage, such
as sustained use discounts

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.120
and committed use discounts.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.820
For example, if you have a lot
of consistent compute engine

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:46.720
usage, you may see a big impact
from sustained use discounts.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.760
Sometimes you need to
drill in to see what's

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.530
driving a spike in your cost.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.220
In this particular
billing account,

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.910
we can see that some pretty big
spikes have occurred recently.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:57.760
By hovering over the chart, it
looks like one specific project

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:58.870
is responsible.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.580
The next step is
to drill in further

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.400
to understand what caused it.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.350
We'll filter to just the
specific project responsible

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.300
and then group byproduct
to see more information.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.330
Having a consistent and
clear naming convention

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:12.430
comes in handy here so
that you can track down

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.920
the right information
about the project usage.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.920
For our example, it looks
like a rise in BigQuery usage

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.550
is responsible for the spike,
but we can see even more detail

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.230
if we filter by SKU.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.270
This reveals that
BigQuery analysis

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:27.970
is what's causing the increase,
which is the SKU for running

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.470
queries in BigQuery.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.153
With the GCB project and
specific cause outlined,

00:03:32.153 --> 00:03:33.820
it should be a lot
easier to narrow down

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.030
what action or application
is driving the cost.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:39.160
So the billing reports
let you see your costs

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.990
and drill down
into specific areas

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:44.045
to monitor and investigate
unexpected changes in cost.

00:03:44.045 --> 00:03:45.670
If you're just looking
for a high level

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.640
summary of your costs, try
the cost breakdown report,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.800
which you can also access
from the left hand menu

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.060
from a billing account.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.640
This report lets you see how
discounts and taxes combined

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.580
with your usage costs to arrive
at your final invoice amount

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:58.750
every month.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.280
Using these
different reports can

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.480
show you a clear picture of your
costs for different scenarios.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.230
Using the billing
reports available to you

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:06.850
directly within the
console, you can

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.320
see what you're
currently spending,

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.510
visualize the amount
your forecasted to spend,

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.490
and understand
your cost drivers.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:13.990
In the next video,
we'll look at how

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.450
to export your billing
data for further analysis

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:17.860
and create custom dashboards.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

